[Music]  this morning on the dish gage and toner  and to put things into perspective  that's a restaurant's name whose diners  were already familiar with it back in  the late 1800s the eatery was an  institution in brooklyn new york for  well over a century pre-dating the  brooklyn bridge the statue of liberty  and even the city subway system it's  seen plenty of ups and downs including  two worldwide pandemics and it's our  current one that delayed its much  anticipated reopening almost two years  ago but now it's back in business once  again  the new restaurant in town has a line  out the door  with people angling for the raw bar  vintage cocktails and the old world  atmosphere on the back 31 porter 31 rib  spinach mashed fries and sprouts the  food in chef adam shepherd's kitchen is  reeling them in too strip  crab cake onion rings  what do you mean  it's the long story exactly what long  time friends and owners sinchin frizzell  ben schneider and zoe kim had hoped for  when he shared a meal before the  official re-opening of gage and told us  this is gorgeous all of this describe  what we're seeing in front of us all  right we have a classic caesar salad  right here this is our steak aged 28  days this is our house made parker house  rolls a classic wedge salad classic very  classic okay another classic staple here  cream spinach  they were celebrating the rebirth of a  brooklyn landmark that it shuttered 17  years ago it was like heartbreaking for  the borough and i think there's a lot of  civic  pride involved in the reopening and it's  not about the three of us it's about  this beautiful  place you know getting the doors back  open here it's like reopening a library  or something it's like public  institution  opened in  1879 charles gage and eugene tolner  moved their restaurant to this fulton  street location in 1892 and for 125  years it stayed true to its oyster chop  house roots even as it passed through  multiple sets of owners and chefs  including culinary legend edna lewis but  in 2004 all of that came to an end do  you know why it closed the dining  patterns were changing in brooklyn it  was a little bit of a ghost town down  here that's when frizzell discovered it  it was sort of hidden away  boarded up what made you spy on it the  last time we knew about this place it  was a clothing store you couldn't see  the walls you couldn't really see the  chandeliers but he could see the  potential an historic space he hoped to  buy with the owners of another brooklyn  eatery the good fork i found out one of  the former owners was a regular customer  of mine at the good fork and i called  him up he advised me against it why for  good reason i was too young i didn't  know i wasn't ready a decade would go by  before the time was finally right i  think a lot of people are very nostalgic  the fact that a lot of old institutions  are closing and for us the way that we  found this place it was perfectly  preserved because it was landmarked  landmarked in 1975  the third interior landmark next to  grant's tomb and public library start  rolling schneider took on the  restoration from its original revolving  door to the once gas-powered lamps we  had to of course clean them up and make  them glow were there any surprises and  then we found hatch door at the very top  of the building i opened it up and then  in there was a whole bunch of this  historical stuff that we found old menus  for me it was actually looking at all  these old menus from a kitchen  standpoint and or looking at a menu that  would represent gage and toner in the  21st century it was  so exciting to look back at the i don't  know the oldest menu going back to  19 yeah 19 19 19.  but three days after this lunch  interview the pandemics shut down nyc  and much of the world  for that entire year i've been wondering  are they coming back did that thought  ever we were wondering too  i knew we would figure out a way i mean  you can't come that far and then not  somehow make it  and a year later one of new york's  oldest continuously run restaurants made  the comeback of comebacks  this moment has to be a great exhale  yeah oh yeah how good does this feel it  feels great when we opened again we were  open at 50  and no seating at the bar and all the  servers and masks but it was like  magical and it's been magical ever since  the resurrection here also gave life to  the sunken harbor club  an upscale speakeasy just upstairs  literally every day we're talking to  people that used to eat here and have  fun memories of the place and them  saying  this is better than we  remembered it thank you so much for not  letting this one go next hands are  coming up to the window right now chef  and that includes the owner that tried  to talk them out of it  and he said  nicely done  did he really he's been a big supporter  yeah i think he's proud of us now we  just got to get brock and michelle to  come  [Laughter]  so great to have you back here  thank you for coming

